# Employing Lead Thiocyanate Additive to Reduce the Hysteresis and Boost the Fill Factor of Planar Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Lead thiocyanate in the perovskite precursor can increase the grain size of a perovskite thin film and reduce the conductivity of the grain boundaries, leading to perovskite solar cells with reduced hysteresis and enhanced fill factor. A planar perovskite solar cell with grain boundary and interface passivation achieves a steady-state efficiency of 18.42%.

Organic–inorganic lead halide perovskite absorbers have attracted significant attention due to their excellent photovoltaic properties, such as an extremely high optical absorption coefficient, very long carrier lifetime, and tunable bandgap.[1–8] The power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells has rapidly increased from $3.8\%$ to over $21\%$ in recent years.[9–17] Despite the rapid improvement of cell efficiency, perovskite solar cells are still facing some challenges, such as moistureinduced instability and the anomalous hysteresis behavior.[18,19] Hysteretic behavior is particularly severe for planar perovskite solar cells with the regular cell structure.[20,21] The photovoltaic parameters of perovskite solar cells depend on the scan rate and scan direction of the photocurrent density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ measurements.[22] Stabilized power output measurement under working conditions has therefore been reported as a better method to estimate the efficiency of perovskite devices.[19]
The origins of the hysteresis have been linked to chargetrapping,[18,22,23] ion migration,[18,24–26] capacitive,[26,27] and ferroelectric effects.[28] A recent report has attributed the primary origins of hysteresis to ion migration through perov­ skite absorbers and trapping of electronic carriers at the interfaces and grain boundaries (GBs).[18] It has been shown that ion migration in lead halide perovskites may be mainly through GBs.[29] Therefore, increasing the grain size and improving the crystalline quality of perovskite films are effective approaches to reduce the hysteresis of perovskite solar cells.[20,26] However, while this approach can reduce GB trap density, it cannot effectively mitigate the interface trap-induced hysteresis. It has been reported that fullerenes can effectively passivate electron selective layer (ESL)/perovskite interfaces and reduce the hysteresis of perovskite devices.[23,30] Therefore, both grain size enhancement and interface passivation may be required to eliminate the hysteretic behavior of planar perovskite solar cells.
There are reports on efficient and hysteresis-free perovskite solar cells.[16,17,23,31–33] These solar cells usually have perovskite absorbers with large grain sizes and use either fullerene passivation or mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ESLs to enhance charge transfer at the ESL/perovskite interface. Metal oxides coated with fullerenes have been reported as good ESLs for perovskite solar cells with less hysteresis, due to the fullerene-promoted charge transfer at the interface.[34–39] Various approaches, including solvent engineering,[40] solvent annealing,[33] solvent bath treatment,[41,42] incorporating additives,[32,43] and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ gastreatment,[44,45] have been used to increase the grain size and crystalline quality of perovskite films.[46] Recently, reports have shown that adding pseudo halogen elements can enhance the moisture-resistivity and quality of perovskite thin films.[47–50] In these reports, perovskites are assumed to be composed of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}\mathrm{I}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3-x}(\mathrm{SCN})_{x}.$ . Solar cells using these perovskites achieved a record efficiency of $11.07\%$ .[47]
In this paper, we report a one-step solution process for perovskite film deposition and demonstrate that adding a small amount of lead thiocyanate $(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2})$ in the perovskite precursor can significantly increase the grain size and crystalline quality of perovskite thin films. However, in contrast to earlier reports, we find no significant $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ ion incorporation into the resulting final perovskite films. We hypothesize that during the perovskite film formation, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ cations and $\mathrm{{SCN^{-}}}$ anions react and form HSCN and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ gases (as a result of the very low stability of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{SCN}$ adduct). The latter gas is known to enlarge the grain size and crystalline quality of perovskite thin films.[48,49] The hypothesis is supported by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (ToF-SIMS), X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements, and density-functional theory (DFT) calculation of the lattice constants of prospective SCN-incorporated perovskites. After the release of HSCN and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ gases, the excess $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ form $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at the GBs, which passivates GBs and reduces dark current through the GBs. Combined with the use of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /fullerene (phenyl- $C_{61}$ -butyric acid methyl ester, PCBM, a popular interface passivation material[23,51]) ESLs, the steadystate efficiency of our best-performing planar perovskite solar cell has improved from $15.57\%$ to $18.42\%$ . Using conventional reverse and forward voltage scanning, PCEs of $19.45\%$ and $18.53\%$ , respectively, were also registered for the champion device. Therefore, our results suggest that using ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additives in the perovskite precursors combined with a fullerene layer at the ESL/perovskite interface is a viable approach for reducing the hysteresis and improving the performance of planar-architecture perovskite solar cells.
Our planar perovskite solar cells have the regular superstrate cell structure, consisting of FTO-coated glass as the substrate, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as the ESL, perovskite $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ $(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})$ with or without ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive in the precursor as the absorber, $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis-(N,N-di-p-methoxyphenylamine)- $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD) as the hole selective layer
