(HSL), and Au as the metal electrode. We have tested the effects of varying the concentration of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the perovskite precursor and find that adding $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ (molar ratio with respect to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}^{\phantom{}}$ ) in the perovskite precursor leads to the best cell performance. Figure 1a shows the $J{-}V$ curves of two representative planar $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ESL-based solar cells using perovskite absorbers with and without $5\%$ $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ in the precursor under reverse and forward voltage scanning. The cell using a perovskite absorber without ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ in the precursor achieved a PCE of 16.41 $(13.21)\%$ , with an open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of 1.09 (1.00) V, a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of 21.55 (21.63) mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a fill factor (FF) of 69.97 $(60.90)\%$ , a series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ of 5.4 (8.6) $\Omega{\mathrm{~cm}}^{2}$ , and a shunt resistance $(R_{\mathrm{sh}})$ of 3200.0 (1404.2) $\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ when measured under reverse (forward) voltage scanning, showing a large degree of hysteresis. The cell using the perovskite absorber with $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ in the precursor achieved a PCE of 18.15 $(17.27)\%$ , with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 1.11 (1.09) V, a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of 21.43 (21.39) mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a FF of 76.35 $(74.35)\%$ , a $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ of 4.2 (4.8) $\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , and a $R_{\mathrm{sh}}$ of 3520.0 (2234.0) $\Omega{\mathrm{~cm}}^{2}$ when measured under reverse (forward) voltage scanning, revealing a significantly reduced degree of hysteresis and a much improved FF. The $J{-}V$ curves measured under different voltage scanning directions and rates are shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information), revealing consistent results.
Figure 1b shows the measured external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of the cells using perovskite absorbers with and without a $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive in the precursor. The integrated current densities based on these curves are 20.42 and $19.33\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for the cells using perovskite absorbers with and without $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ in the precursor, respectively. While the values of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ taken from the $J{-}V$ curves for the two devices are the same, to within the degree of certainty of the measurement, the current densities derived from the integrated QE curves yield a slightly higher value for the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ -treated sample. This also suggests that the absorber without $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive in the precursor may have a higher trap state density than the absorber with $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive in the precursor. The EQE measurements were conducted without an AM1.5G light bias. It is understandable that the integrated currents from the QE curves are in general lower than that obtained from the $J{-}V$ measurements. The cells with a higher density of trap states will exhibit lower QE.
Figure 1c displays the histogram data of the PCEs of 30 solar cells, prepared using perovskite absorbers without $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ in the precursor and measured under reverse and forward voltage scanning. The average $V_{\mathrm{oc}},\ J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ FF, and PCE for these 30 solar cells are $1.05\pm0.03$ $(0.98\pm0.04)\$ V, $21.31\pm$ $0.46\ (21.24\pm0.48)\ \mathrm{mAcm^{-2}}$ , $70.98\pm2.16$ $(64.51\pm3.34)\%$ , and $15.91~\pm~0.62$ ( $13.38~\pm~0.77\%$ , respectively, measured under reverse (forward) voltage scanning. Figure 1d displays the histogram data of the PCEs of 30 solar cells, prepared using perovskite absorbers with $5\%\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ in the precursor and measured under reverse and forward voltage scanning. The average $V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ $J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ FF, and PCE for these 30 solar cells measured under reverse (forward) voltage scanning are $1.09\pm0.02$ $(1.06\pm0.03)\$ V, 21.25 $\pm\:0.82$ $21.16\pm0.81$ ) $\mathrm{\mAcm}^{-2}$ , $76.33\pm2.05$ $(73.66\pm2.36)\%$ , and $17.62\pm0.46$ ( $16.58\pm0.68)\%$ , respectively. It is seen that, with the exception of the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values (which are essentially equivalent in the two cases), all photovoltaic para­meters, especially the FF, have been improved and the degree of hysteresis has been reduced when a $5\%$ $\mathrm{\;\Pb(SCN)\it_{2}}$ additive was introduced to the perovskite precursor. We have also measured the steady-state efficiency of the two representative cells shown in Figure 1a by applying a constant bias voltage taken from the average maximum power points in the $J{-}V$ measurements with both reverse and forward scanning direction. Figure 1e,f show that the steady-state efficiency of the cells using perovskite absorbers with and without a $5\%$ $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the precursor are $17.80\%$ and $15.57\%$ , respectively. It is clear that adding $5\%$ $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ in the perovskite precursor significantly improves the steady-state efficiency of perovskite solar cells.
We now discuss the possible origins for the reduced hysteresis and improved FF in perovskite solar cells induced by the $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the perovskite precursor. We found that a small amount of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive can significantly increase the grain size of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ thin films. Figure 2a,b shows the top-view scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of perovskite films without and with $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive in the precursor, respectively, deposited on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ESLs. The $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ thin film without ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ in the precursor exhibits grain size of ${\approx}100{\-}400\ \mathrm{nm}$ . However, the $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}$ thin film with $5\%$ ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ in the precursor exhibits an average grain size of ${\approx}2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . It is important to note that both $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite thin films with and without $5\%$ $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\operatorname{Pb}(\mathsf{S C N})_{2}$ in the precursor have very smooth surfaces and similar film thicknesses $(\approx550\ \mathrm{nm})$ , as shown in the cross-sectional SEM images (Figure 2c,d). For the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ thin film with $5\%$ $\mathrm{\Delta,\Pb(SCN)_{2}}$ additive in the precursor, the average grain width is approximately four times larger than the film thickness. The large aspect ratio is highly desirable for polycrystalline thin-film photovoltaic applications because GBs in $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ thin films are known to be trap centers.[31] A higher aspect ratio should lead to reduced dark current and, therefore, improved $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ and FF. This provides one explanation for the performance improvement induced by the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive seen in Figure 1. The higher aspect ratio is also desirable for reducing the hysteresis associated with the GBs.
The top-view SEM image, shown in Figure 2b, reveals higher contrast along GBs, suggesting the formation of secondary phase(s) at the GBs. XRD studies reveal the presence of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ as a secondary phase in the films processed with ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ , which suggests that this secondary phase at the GBs is $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (no other secondary phases were observed in the diffraction pattern). This is further confirmed by SEM-based X-ray energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) analysis. As shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information), the EDS spectrum taken from a grain interior shows a $\mathrm{Pb}$ to I atomic ratio of 1:2.94, close to the ratio in $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , whereas the EDS taken from a secondary phase region shows a $\mathrm{Pb}$ to I atomic ratio of 1:2.22, close to the ratio in $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . We have taken top-view SEM images (Figure S3, Supporting Information) and XRD patterns (Figure 3a) from $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ thin films with various concentrations of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ in the perovskite precursors: $0\%$ , $2.5\%$ , $5\%$ , $7.5\%$ , and $10\%$ . As shown in Figure S3 (Supporting Information), the grain size increases as the amount of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ increases. The accumulation of the secondary phase at the GBs also increases as the amount of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ increases. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ XRD impurity peak intensity increases with the concentration of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ in the precursor (Figure  3a); however, there are no diffraction peaks for leftover excess ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ phase. In addition, the peak positions of the (110) and the (220) planes of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ do not change as the amount of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ increases. An increase in cell volume and therefore a shift in XRD peak positions to lower angle would be expected with increasing SCN substitution based on our DFT calculation results, as shown in Figure S4 (Supporting Information). Since this is not observed, we suspect that most SCN ions do not remain in the final perovskite thin film, unlike the results reported in recent literatures.[47–50,52]
Our speculation regarding loss of the SCN ion is confirmed by XPS and FTIR measurements. As shown in Figure S5 (Supporting Information), the XPS spectra taken from a final perovskite thin film with $5\%$ $\%\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the precursor does not show any evidence of sulfur. The FTIR spectra of thin films of pure $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ , pure $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}$ , $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ with $5\%$ $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the precursor (with and without) annealing are shown in Figure S6 (Supporting Information). The peak related to SCN ions is clearly seen in the spectrum taken from the perovskite film with $5\%$ $\mathrm{\Delta,\Pb(SCN)_{2}}$ additive in the precursor, when the film is not annealed. However, this peak is absent in the spectrum taken from the final perovskite film, suggesting that most $\mathrm{{SCN^{-}}}$ ions do not remain in the final film after annealing. To verify whether or not a small amount of residual $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ ion may still remain in the final film, the unannealed and final films are measured by ToF-SIMS, a technique with a detection limit of as low as parts per billion (ppb). The ToF-SIMS depth profiles shown in Figure S7 (Supporting Information) indicate that the average sulfur concentration in a final perovskite film is likely in the range of $10^{19}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , below the detection limit of XPS and FTIR. Therefore, only a trace amount of $\mathrm{SCN^{-1}}$ ions remain in the final film, which are not expected to cause detectable effects on the structural, optical, and electrical properties of the film. We hypothesize that the following reaction occurs during the fabrication of our perovskite thin films:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}I}+\mathrm{PbI_{2}}+x\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}\rightarrow(1-2x)\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}}}\\ &{\qquad+3x\mathrm{PbI_{2}}+2x(\mathrm{HSCN}\uparrow+\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{2}}\uparrow)}\end{array}
$$
The formation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ gas during the formation of the perovskite films could be responsible for the enlarged grain size and improved crystalline quality of the perovskite films, similar to that reported in the literature.[44,45] The accompanying formation of HSCN gas leads to the loss of most SCN ions from the film. Therefore, the $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additives in the precursors would only improve the crystalline quality and result in excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ formed at the GBs. The excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at the GBs may passivate GBs and exhibit beneficial effects on cell performance, if the layer of this secondary phase does not get too thick, as reported by others.[32,53,54] Due to the enlarged grain size, improved crystalline quality, and passivation of GBs, the perovskite film with $5\%\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the precursor exhibited much longer minority carrier lifetime $(1.9\upmu\mathrm{s})$ than the perovskite film without additive in the precursor $(1.0~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ , as measured by time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Though the ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additives in the precursors do not alter the bandgap of the perovskite absorbers, too much excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at GBs can reduce the absorbance of the perovskite films as shown in Figure 3b, which is not desirable for achieving high $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . As shown in Figure S9 and Table S1 (Supporting Information), the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ decreases as the amount of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ increases. Therefore, there is a trade-off between the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF (increase) and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ (decrease) when adding ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ in the perovskite precursor. The optimal amount of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive can depend on the specific growth process and cell configuration. Our device results reveal that about $5\%\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive gives the optimal device performance.
Conductive atomic-force microscopy (c-AFM) measurements further indicate that the formation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at the GBs reduces conductivity (combined electronic and ionic) along GBs. Figure 4a shows the c-AFM image taken from a perovskite thin film without $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ in the precursor and with an applied bias of $0.25~\mathrm{V}.$ The brighter contrast indicates a higher local current. It is seen that, even though some grains show higher current than others, the GBs generally show higher currents than the adjacent grains. This is seen more clearly from the line profile shown in Figure $\scriptstyle4C$ , with the corresponding scan path shown in Figure 4a. The c-AFM image taken from the perovskite thin film with a $5\%\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the precursor, with the same applied bias, shows a very different contrast between the grains and the GBs. As seen in Figure 4b, the GB regions show even lower current than the grain interiors. The features are seen more clearly in the line profile shown in Figure 4d. The corresponding AFM images of Figure $^{4\mathrm{a},\mathrm{b}}$ are shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information). There is no direct correlation between the current and AFM images, suggesting that the observed difference in GB features is not due to artificial topographic effects.
It should be noted that the current route of c-AFM spreads out from the point right below the probe. The current density decreases rapidly with increasing distance from the contact. Therefore, the current density flowing through the nanometerscale probe-sample contact is very large. This current density is very different from the dark current density measured in a normal device with a macroscopic contact. Although the current route of c-AFM spreads out, the resistance is dominated by the nm-scale volume of the sample below the probe, resulting in nm-resolution resistivity mapping. Therefore, our c-AFM investigations reveal that the segregation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at the GBs reduces the dark current/conductivity along the GBs in perov­ skite thin films. The measured conductivity may be a combination of electronic and ionic conductivity. Because $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ has a higher bandgap $(\approx2.3\ \mathrm{eV})^{[55]}$ and a larger activation energy for ion conduction $(\approx0.53\ \mathrm{eV})^{[56]}$ than $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , i.e., $1.57~\mathrm{eV}^{[57]}$ and $0.2\mathrm{-}0.4\ \mathrm{eV},^{[58]}$ respectively, it is likely that the formation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at GBs should reduce both the electronic and ionic conductivities at GBs, consistent with the c-AFM results. Therefore, the enlarged grain size (reduced GB volume) and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ passivation of GBs are likely responsible for the reduced hysteresis and improved FF for the cells grown on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ESLs using perov­ skite absorbers with $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the precursor.
The hysteretic behavior and the photovoltaic performance of perovskite solar cells are related to both the grain size of the perovskite films and charge recombination or barriers at the perovskite/ESL interfaces.[26] It has been reported that fullerenes can effectively promote charge transfer and passivate interfaces and GBs of perovskites.[33,59–62] Therefore, in order to further reduce the degree of hysteresis and improve cell performance, we combined the use of a ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ additive in the perovskite precursor with a fullerene interface layer. We fabricated planar solar cells using perovskite thin films with $5\%$ $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ in the precursor and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{PCBM}$ ESLs (in the place of the single layer $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ESL). Figure 5a shows the $J{-}V$ curves of the best-performing planar perovskite solar cell. This cell achieved a PCE of 19.45 $(18.53)\%$ , with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 1.11 (1.06) V, a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of 22.44 (22.46) $\mathrm{\mAcm}^{-2}$ , and a FF of 78.22 $(77.89)\%$ when measured under reverse (forward) voltage scanning. The bestperforming cell also achieved a steady-state efficiency of $18.42\%$ with a steady-state current density of $20.22\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at a constant bias voltage of 0.911 V (Figure 5b). The average $V_{\mathrm{oc}},\:J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ FF, and PCE for ten solar cells using the combination of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ PCBM ESLs and $5\%$ $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\ \mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ additive in the perovskite precursor, measured under reverse (forward) voltage scanning, are $1.10\pm0.02$ $(1.07\pm0.01)\$ V, $21.83\pm0.96$ $(21.82\pm0.97)$ mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $78.12\pm1.18$ $(75.90\pm2.59)\%$ , and $18.70\pm0.86$ $(17.67\pm1.01)\%$ , respectively. It is seen that the use of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{PCBM}$ ESLs helps to further improve the device performance and reduce the degree of hysteresis. Therefore, to produce high performance perovskite solar cells, combined engineering of GBs and interfaces is necessary. It is noted that our solar cells still exhibit a small amount of hysteresis. It is likely that the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ESL and the HSLs used in these cells are not fully optimized yet, leaving rooms for further optimization of the cell performance.
## Experimental Section
Device Fabrication: $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ESLs were prepared by a low-temperature solution process described in previous work:[63,64] the solution of 0.1 mol $\mathsf{L}^{-1}\mathsf{S n C l}_{2}$ (Alfa, anhydrous $99.9985\%$ ) dissolved in ethanol was spin-coated on clean FTO substrates (TEC 15, Hartford Glass Co) with a spin rate of 2000 rpm for $30\varsigma$ . The films were subsequently annealed on a hotplate in air at $185^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 1 h. $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}/\mathsf{P C B M}$ ESLs were prepared by spincoating $10\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ PCBM dichlorobenzene solutions on the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ films with a spin rate of 2000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ and then annealing on a hotplate at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 10 min in a glove box. Compact perovskite absorber layers were deposited on the ESLs by using a solvent engineering method described in the literature.[65] The perovskite films without ${\mathsf{P b}}({\mathsf{S C N}})_{2}$ in the precursor were prepared by spin-coating the precursor solution consisting of $467\ m g$ of $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ and $\boldsymbol{159}\mathrm{\mg}$ of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}|$ dissolved in $723~\upmu\upiota$ of N,N-dimethylformamide and $87~\upmu\up L$ of dimethyl sulfoxide. The molar ratio between $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}|$ is 1:1. The perovskite films with $2.5\%$ , $5\%$ , $7.5\%$ , and $10\%$ ${\mathsf{P b}}({\mathsf{S C N}})_{2}$ (Sigma, $99.5\%$ in the precursor were prepared by spin-coating the above-mentioned solution but with 8, 16, 24, and $32~\mathsf{m g}$ of $P b(S C N)_{2}$ , respectively, added to the perovskite precursor solution. All the perovskite films with and without ${\mathsf{P b}}({\mathsf{S C N}})_{2}$ in the precursor were annealed at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 2 min and then $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 5 min in a glovebox. The HSL solution consisted of $26\times10^{-3}$ m Li-bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl) imide (LiTFSI) (Sigma, $99.95\%)$ , $55\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{{M}}$ 4-tert-butylpyridine (TBP) (Sigma, $96\%$ , and $68\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{{u}}$ spiro-OMeTAD (Shenzhen Feiming Science and Technology Co., Ltd., $99.0\%$ , which were dissolved in a mixed solvent of acetonitrile (Sigma, $99.8\%$ and chlorobenzene (Sigma, $99.8\%$ with a volume ratio of 1:10. The HSL solution was spin-coated on the perovskite films at 500 rpm for 1 s and subsequently 2000 rpm for 60 s in a dry box. To complete the devices, $60\ n m$ thick Au electrodes were thermally evaporated on top of the HSLs. The active area of the solar cells was $0.08~\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ .
Film and Device Characterization: Absorbance spectra were measured by a UV–vis spectrophotometer (PerkinElmer Lambda 1050). The crystallinity and phase purity of perovskite films were examined by XRD (Rigaku Ultima III), with a $\mathsf{C u K}_{\alpha}$ radiation source and with the operation conditions of $40~\mathsf{k V}$ and $44~\mathsf{m A}$ . The perovskite film composition was characterized by an XPS system (PHI Quantum 2000) and a ToF-SIMS V (ION TOF). FTIR spectra were measured by a Thermo Scientific Nicolet 6700 FT-IR spectrometer. C-AFM was performed on a Veeco D5000 AFM in a glovebox equipped with the Nanoscope V controller. A nanosensor PPP-EFM tip was used in the contact mode for AFM measurement. The morphology of the devices and the perovskite films were characterized by a high-resolution field emission SEM (Hitachi S-4800). TRPL spectra were measured by exciting samples using a supercontinuum fiber laser at a wavelength of $500~\mathsf{n m}$ , repetition rate of $0.1~\mathsf{M H z}$ and $5~\upmu\upwarrow$ laser power with the spot size being focused down to $300~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\times300~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Maximum emission was collected at a wavelength of $775\:\:\mathsf{n m}$ on a single-photon counter. External quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra were taken on a QE system (PV Measurements Inc.). $J-V$ curves with a voltage settling time of $\mathsf{10}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{s}$ were measured by a Keithley Model 2400 under AM1.5G simulated irradiation with a standard solar simulator (PV Measurements Inc.). The light intensity of the solar simulator was calibrated by a standard silicon solar cell provided by PV Measurements Inc.
DFT Calculations: The DFT calculations were performed using the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP)[66,67] code with the standard frozen-core projector augmented-wave (PAW)[68,69] method. The generalized gradient approximation (GGA) of Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE)[70] functional was used for exchange-correlation. Substitutional $\mathsf{M A}_{16}\mathsf{P b}_{16}\mathsf{I}_{48-n}(\mathsf{S C N})_{n}$ $(n~=~0,1,2,4,$ models in a $2\times2\times1$ supercell of tetragonal ${\beta\mathrm{{\cdot}M A P b}}|_{3}$ were used for the doping structures. The cutoff energy of plane wave basis set was set to 500 eV. A $2\times2\times2$ k-mesh was used for sampling the Brillouin zone. All atoms were relaxed until the Hellmann–Feynman forces on them were below $0.01\ \mathrm{eV}\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ .
